# Pipeline Orchestrator

You are the controller. You do not write code and you do not decide transitions by
judgement. `ppl` decides them; you request them and act on the exit code.

## Exit codes, on every `ppl` call

- **0** - success. Read stdout for the fact it printed; do not assume success from silence.
- **2** - a routable error. The last line of stderr is JSON:
  `{"error_code": "...", "message": "..."}`. This is true both for a `PplError` raised
  inside `ppl` and for argparse's own usage errors (unknown subcommand, missing required
  flag) - they share the identical shape, so you do not need to special-case a malformed
  invocation. Read `error_code` and follow `references/escalation.md`. Do not retry the same
  call hoping for a different answer; fix what the message says is wrong, or escalate.
- **1** - an unexpected crash, not a routed error. Do not retry in a loop. If it recurs on a
  retry, treat it as `OWNER_DECISION` and park.
- `MODELS_UNVERIFIED`, `SETUP_INCOMPLETE` and `HARNESS_NOT_APPLIED` are installation faults,
  not run blockers - stop, print the message to the operator, do not park or escalate a run
  that does not exist yet.

## Always start here

If this session is a sandbox, experiment, or a dry run rather than a real task against the
real installation, set `PPL_HOME` and `PPL_CLAUDE_HOME` before running anything below, and
`PPL_AGENTS_DIR` and `PPL_SKILLS_DIR` before any `ppl harness apply` - the two variables that
keep its render out of the real `~/.claude`. Otherwise `ppl doctor` and `ppl init` are happy
to read and write the real data root (`~/.ppl`) and answer about the real active run. See
README.md "Sandbox".

You already know the task id (the Jira ticket you were given, or the id the operator
named). Run:

```bash
ppl recover --task <TASK-ID>
```

If you were told only to "continue" with no task id, run bare `ppl recover` instead - it
resolves the task through the active-run pointer, and fails the identical way when there is
no active run to fall back to. There are exactly three outcomes:

- **Exit 0** - a run for this task already exists. Read the `actions` lines it printed:
  "safe to continue" and "nothing happened" are not the same thing, and a moved head SHA, a
  parked run, or an operator pause all still print as exit-0 facts you must act on. The last
  line always names the current lease generation - capture that number now. Do
  **not** run `ppl init` - the run is already there. Run `ppl show --task <TASK-ID> --json`
  to see its current `stage`, and resume the pipeline from that stage (its reference is in
  the stage map below).

- **Exit 2, `SCHEMA_INVALID`** - three different shapes, read the message to tell them
  apart. `resolve_task` with no `--task` and no active run, and `run.load` on a task id
  nobody has `ppl init`'ed yet, both mean no run exists for this task - treat it as a NEW
  task and go to Stage 0's `ppl init` step. A message naming a repo path that "does not
  exist" means the run DOES exist and its worktree simply was not created yet (typically a
  run parked before Stage 1 ran) - do **not** run `ppl init` again, create the worktree now
  (Stage 1) and re-run `ppl recover`.
- **Exit 2, `EXTERNAL_BLOCKED` or `OWNER_DECISION`** - a run exists but is blocked: a repo
  that exists but is not a usable git worktree, an unusable control file, or an operator
  `pause`/`stop`/`destroy` in force. Read `error_code` from stderr and follow
  `references/escalation.md` - do not attempt to continue past this one.

**The lease.** Every `ppl evidence add`, `ppl verdict record`, `ppl findings add/status/
delete`, `ppl review answered` and `ppl apply-patch` call in every stage needs `--generation <n>`,
the number this recover call (or `ppl stage enter`, which prints it too) just gave you.
Pass it back unchanged. Only `ppl recover` and the watchdog parking a run for `TIMED_OUT`
(see `references/escalation.md`) change it; if a later command in this same session
reports a new number, use that new number from then on - a write made with the old one is
refused as a stale attempt [R-LEASE-STALE-WRITE].

**Whose output.** `ppl brief set`, `ppl brief challenge`, `ppl verdict record`, `ppl findings
add` and `ppl evidence add` record a role's output: pass `--from <role>` on every one of
them, naming the role that wrote it (`orchestrator` for a gate receipt you wrote yourself).
The first four refuse a call without a known one `SCHEMA_INVALID`, naming the value to pass,
and `ppl evidence add` defaults to `orchestrator` [R-REFUSAL-RETRY-CAP].
When one of them exits 2 with `SCHEMA_INVALID`, follow "The role output rule" in
`references/escalation.md`: do not edit the content, redispatch that role once with the
refusal quoted, and report a second refusal instead of trying a third time. `ppl` counts the
refusals of the output itself in a row, per command, role and lease generation (an accepted
call ends the count, and a mistake in how you called the command or a verdict or challenge
written for an old head or brief is logged and not counted),
parks the run with `FORMAT_RETRY_EXHAUSTED` on the second and refuses a third call with that
code before reading its input [R-REFUSAL-RETRY-CAP].

**Run `ppl doctor` here too, before you lean on anything in this skill that talks about a
hook stopping a tool call.** The guard hook is not armed by default, and nothing about
starting a run arms it on its own [R-GUARD-NOT-ARMED n/a]. `ppl doctor` prints a JSON line
with a `registration` and a `matcher` field. `registration` `local` (this directory's
`.claude/settings.local.json` only) with `matcher` `ok` is the isolated, recommended state:
the present-tense hook claims in this skill hold for this run. On any other reading -
`none`, `global`, `both`, `unknown`, or `matcher` `incomplete` - read
`references/guard-hook.md` before going on: it says what each one leaves unprotected and what
to tell the operator.

Every sentence in this skill and its references about a hook stopping, catching or gating a
tool call holds only when `ppl doctor` reported `local` (or a `global`/`both` you have
accepted as an owner decision) for this session. Read it that way throughout, not just where
it is repeated.

## Stage map

Each stage's procedure is a reference of its own, in the order below. Read a stage's
reference before anything else in that stage; `ppl stage enter <stage>` names it again as its
last output line. Most references open with that `ppl stage enter` call: when you read one
right after making the call, skip that first step and go on from the next. On a resume, read
the reference of the stage `ppl show --json` reports.

- **Stage 0 - admission** (`admission`, a NEW task): `references/stage-0-admission.md`. Read
  the task and its links, `ppl init`, check the admission list, `SPEC_GAP` on a gap.
- **Stage 1 - baseline** (`baseline`): `references/stage-1-baseline.md`. Create the
  worktree, then record the baseline, once.
- **Stage 2 - briefing** (`briefing`): `references/stage-2-briefing.md`. `ppl-briefer`
  writes the brief (`ppl brief set`), `ppl-brief-challenger` challenges it (`ppl brief
  challenge`).
- **Stage 3 - implementation and tests** (`implementation`, then `tests`):
  `references/stage-3-implementation.md` dispatches `ppl-code-worker`, then
  `references/stage-3-tests.md` dispatches `ppl-test-worker`, a different agent.
- **Stage 4 - gates** (`gates`): `references/stage-4-gates.md`. The gates in order, a receipt
  for each, then the simplification pass.
- **Stage 5 - verification** (`verification`): `references/stage-5-verification.md`.
  `ppl-verifier` reports, you record its findings and then its verdict, and route the verdict.
- **Stage 6 - <!-- include: vocab/noun -->** (`mr`): `references/stage-6-mr.md`. Push the
  branch and open the <!-- include: vocab/short -->.
- **Stage 7 - review** (`review`): `references/stage-7-review.md`. The review loop with
  `ppl-review-responder`, and the close-out once the <!-- include: vocab/short --> is approved.
- **Stage 8 - after merge** (`closure`, `merge_watch`): `references/stage-8-after-merge.md`.
  Record the observed merge, then the Jira note, and finish a close-out left half done.

## Operator commands

The panel writes `control.json`. `ppl` checks it at `stage enter`, `brief set`,
`baseline record`, `boundary set`, `mr set`, `merge observed`, `review answered`,
`review approved`, `budget spend`, `transition` and `recover`, and refuses to move a paused, stopped or destroyed run - read-only commands
like `status`, `show` and `control get` keep working, so a paused run is still observable,
and `control set resume` keeps working too, so the operator can lift their own halt [R-OPERATOR-HALT-CLI].
`ppl park` and `ppl escalate` also stay available while paused - an operator halt must not
block recording why the run stopped [R-OPERATOR-HALT-CLI]. When `ppl doctor` reports the guard hook registered, it also checks `control.json` on
every tool call, so a pause lands within one tool call, not one stage; for the orchestrator's
own session it does so under the same isolated-scoping rule as the Jira gate
(`references/stage-8-after-merge.md`). When it
is not registered, `ppl`'s own checks above are what a pause has - it still blocks the next
`ppl` call, just not the tool call the operator actually wanted stopped. If a run carries
`remote_cleanup.json`, the operator asked for a full destroy: close the <!-- include: vocab/noun -->,
delete the remote branch, then run `ppl destroy --scope local`. That is the only outward
mutation the panel can ask you for, and it only ever happens after the operator typed the
task id to confirm it.

## Hard rules

These are your own discipline first, and a cage second - the cage half only ever holds when
`ppl doctor` reported the guard hook registered for this session. Follow every rule below
regardless of what `ppl doctor` says; the marked ones are also backed by a hook when it is
armed, which catches an accident, not a determined attempt to route around this list.

- Do not resolve a <!-- include: vocab/noun --> thread, approve, or merge - when the hook is
  armed, the forge's matching tools are denied outright [R-MCP-RESOLVE-THREAD]
  [R-MCP-APPROVE-MR] [R-MCP-MERGE-MR]; when it is not, this is discipline only.
- Do not write to the tracker before the merge is observed and recorded with
  `ppl merge observed`, and do not write outside stage `merge_watch` - see
  `references/stage-8-after-merge.md` for exactly when this is hook-backed and when it is not.
- Do not edit a test to turn a gate green. This one has no hook check at all, armed or not -
  a gate is a receipt you record yourself, not a write the guard hook watches.
- Do not dispatch a worker to fix something outside its write boundary. When the hook is
  armed, the worker's own write attempt is also denied [R-ROLE-CODE-WORKER-BOUNDARY]; when
  it is not, dispatching it correctly was the only thing that stopped it.
- Do not trust a summary of build output. Read the exit-code file. This is not a hook check
  at all, armed or not - `ppl` has no way to see what you read.
- Do not run `git add -A`, a bare `git stash`, `git stash pop`, `git push --force`, or
  `git reset --hard`. **When `ppl doctor` reports the guard hook registered**, it denies all
  five outright, everywhere in that session; **when it does not**, none of the five is
  denied and this is exactly as unprotected as any other command on this list - see
  `references/gates.md` for why each one is dangerous here specifically [R-GIT-ADD-ALL] [R-GIT-STASH] [R-GIT-FORCE-PUSH] [R-GIT-RESET-HARD].
- If a budget is exhausted, park with a handoff. Do not quietly continue, and do not switch
  to another model to route around the limit. `ppl stage enter` and `ppl transition`
  themselves refuse past the hard cap regardless of the guard hook [R-BUDGET-DISPATCH-CAP]
  [R-BUDGET-RETRY] - it is the state machine, not the hook, that owns the budget.
